title: Command classes
tags: Keybindings Configuration
kind: reference
summary: gui/command-classes.toml gives every command its class, A to D or ✕, by how often it is used: what key it may have, and the keybindings panel's filter.

Every command has a ''class'', by how often it is used; the class decides what key
it may have ([[Choosing keybindings]]):

|!Class |!Used |!Gets |
|A |all the time |one key |
|B |several times a session |one modifier, or two keys |
|C |now and then |two or three keys |
|D |rarely |the command input only |
|✕ (`X` in the file) |cannot be undone |never a key |

The classes are in a file of their own, `~/.config/metafolder/gui/command-classes.toml`,
so that `keybindings.toml` stays a list of keys:

```
"log:revert" = "B"
"log:revert op" = "C"
"log:prune" = "X"
```

A key is a command, or the first words of an invocation: `"log:revert"` classes
every revert, `"log:revert op"` that one only, and the longest key that fits an
invocation wins — by whole words, so `"log:revert op"` does not fit
`log:revert opx`. A key given twice, or a class other than `A` `B` `C` `D` `X`, is an
error that names the key; the file is required, like the other files of the GUI
([[No runtime fallback]]). The GUI checks it at startup and reads it again each time
the [[keybindings panel]] asks, so an edit shows when the panel is next shown, or at
`view:refresh`.

The [[keybindings panel]] filters its list by class. The shipped file classes every
shipped command — the commands of `user-commands.js` are yours to class — and a test
holds the shipped keys to it (`test_the_shipped_classes_cover_every_command_and_fit_the_keys`):

* a command of class D or ✕ has no key;
* a letter typed alone, without Shift, runs a command of class A;
* a class A command that has keys has one of a single chord.
